---
// The markup and the manifest are the library's own output, and the byte ranges come from the
// byte manifest CI checks, so this page cannot drift from what a build writes.
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import { manifestPurpose } from '../../../lib/config.mjs';
import { DEFAULT_BG, DEFAULT_SIZES, linkTags, manifestJson } from '../../../lib/core.mjs';
import manifest from '../../../test/bytes.json';

const title = 'Every file in a favicon set, and the HTML it needs - favseed';
const description = 'Each file favseed writes, with its size, format and the HTML it needs, and which browser or platform reads it: tabs, iOS, Android and desktop installs.';
const lede = 'A build writes five files, and a sixth with a manifest. This page lists each one, the markup it needs, and which browsers and platforms read it.';

const marks = manifest.profiles.default.marks;
const range = (file) => {
  const sizes = marks.map((mark) => manifest.files[`default/${mark}/${file}`].bytes).sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const n = (value) => value.toLocaleString('en-GB');
  if (sizes[0] === sizes.at(-1)) return `${n(sizes[0])} B`;
  return `${n(sizes[0])}–${n(sizes.at(-1))} B, median ${n(sizes[(sizes.length - 1) >> 1])}`;
};
const files = [
  ['icon.svg', 'SVG', 'the source’s viewBox', range('icon.svg')],
  ['favicon.ico', 'ICO, one PNG entry', '48×48', range('favicon.ico')],
  ['apple-touch-icon.png', 'PNG, opaque', '180×180', range('apple-touch-icon.png')],
  ...DEFAULT_SIZES.map((size) => [`icon-${size}.png`, 'PNG, opaque', `${size}×${size}`, range(`icon-${size}.png`)]),
  ['site.webmanifest', 'JSON', 'icons only', range('site.webmanifest')],
];
const platforms = [
  ['Browser tabs', '<link rel="icon">', 'none', 'icon.svg, favicon.ico'],
  ['iOS, iPadOS, Safari Add to Dock', 'apple-touch-icon', 'rounded square, corners about 22%', 'apple-touch-icon.png'],
  ['Android (Chrome, WebAPK)', 'manifest, any maskable', 'circle, squircle, rounded square', 'icon-192.png, icon-512.png'],
  ['ChromeOS, macOS (Chrome installs)', 'manifest, any maskable', 'the system’s rounded shape', 'icon-192.png, icon-512.png'],
  ['Windows, Linux desktops', 'manifest, any maskable', 'none: drawn as is, on light and dark', 'icon-192.png, icon-512.png'],
];
const links = linkTags('/', true).trim();
const json = manifestJson({ sizes: DEFAULT_SIZES, purpose: manifestPurpose(DEFAULT_BG) }).trim();
---
<Doc {title} {description} heading="Outputs" {lede}>
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-set">The set</h2>
    <div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Format</th><th>Size</th><th>Bytes on the test marks</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {files.map(([name, format, size, bytes]) => (<tr><td><code>{name}</code></td><td>{format}</td><td>{size}</td><td>{bytes}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><small>Bytes are from the {marks.length} marks CI builds at the default effort, on a black ground and with a
      manifest. The smallest PNGs come from a mark of flat tiles and the largest from a gradient; yours depend on
      your mark. The manifest is the icons-only one shown below.</small></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-markup">The markup</h2>
    <p>Put these tags in your <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. <code>favseed --html --manifest</code> prints them, with
      the config's <code>base</code> in front of every <code>href</code> when the site is served under a path. The
      Vite plugin and the Astro integration add them for you, to any page that does not already link an icon.</p>
    <pre>{links}</pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="iconsvg"><code>icon.svg</code></h2>
    <p>The favicon for browsers that take an SVG icon, and the file to use for the logo on your pages. It keeps
      animation, styling and accessibility, and any <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> block, so a dark-mode rule
      changes the favicon with the browser’s colour scheme. Use the same <code>/icon.svg</code> URL in the link and
      in <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, so one cached response serves both.</p>
    <p>It is not in the manifest. An SVG entry makes Chrome’s Android install fail and fall back to a home-screen
      bookmark (<a href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40925759">crbug.com/40925759</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="faviconico"><code>favicon.ico</code></h2>
    <p>The fallback for browsers that do not take the SVG, and the file a browser asks for
      at <code>/favicon.ico</code> when a page declares no icon. It holds one 48×48 entry: a PNG inside a 22-byte
      container, transparent and unpadded. The link says <code>sizes="48x48"</code>, the size of the bitmap that is
      actually inside.</p>
    <p>Why 48 and not 32: across 20 test marks the larger entry costs about 97 bytes a mark, and the worst error
      against the source falls from 1.660% to 1.215%. A PNG payload rather than a BMP one, because the
      BMP that <code>icotool</code> writes by default is 8.8 times larger. Like every raster, it shows the light
      rules of the SVG; no platform asks a bitmap favicon for a colour scheme.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="apple-touch-iconpng"><code>apple-touch-icon.png</code></h2>
    <p>iOS and iPadOS use it for the Home Screen, and Safari for Add to Dock. It is 180×180, 60 pt at @3x, the largest
      size Apple documents for a web clip; no iPhone renders above @3x. iOS prefers this file to the manifest icons
      and ignores their <code>purpose</code> and any SVG.</p>
    <p>The mark sits in the safe zone on the <code>--bg</code> ground, so the rounded mask, its corners about 22% of
      the tile, cuts nothing from it. It is opaque because iOS would composite a transparent icon onto black anyway.
      Choose the ground to suit the mark: <code>--bg '#fff'</code> for one drawn on light.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="icon-192png-and-icon-512png"><code>icon-192.png</code> and <code>icon-512.png</code></h2>
    <p>The manifest’s icons, declared <code>any maskable</code>. Android masks them to a circle, squircle or rounded
      square, ChromeOS and Chrome installs on macOS apply the system’s rounded shape, and Windows and Linux desktops
      draw them as they are. web.dev’s install criteria, Chrome’s documentation and MDN all ask for a 192 and a
      512 px icon, so those are the default sizes; <code>--sizes</code> takes other even sizes.</p>
    <p>One file carries both purposes. Chrome will not install an app whose icons are all <code>maskable</code>,
      and the mark is padded into the safe zone, so <code>any</code> is true as well. The cost is on desktops that
      do not mask: there the icon shows a smaller mark on its ground, not edge to edge. <code>--bg none</code> opts
      out of all of it, with a transparent, unpadded set declared <code>any</code> only.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="sitewebmanifest"><code>site.webmanifest</code></h2>
    <p>Written with <code>--manifest</code>; the page on this site includes one by default. It lists the padded PNGs and nothing
      else, one entry per line:</p>
    <pre>{json}</pre>
    <p>Merge your own <code>name</code>, <code>short_name</code> and <code>theme_color</code>, or give them in the
      config’s <code>manifest</code> object, which writes them ahead of the icons. With vite-plugin-pwa, which writes
      the manifest itself, pass <code>favseedIcons()</code> to it for the icon entries.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="who-reads-what">Who reads what</h2>
    <div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Reads</th><th>Shape</th><th>File</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {platforms.map(([platform, reads, shape, file]) => (<tr><td>{platform}</td><td>{reads}</td><td>{shape}</td><td><code>{file}</code></td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a> explains the safe zone and how each file is encoded.</p>
  </section>
</Doc>
